<section class="an-hero"><div class="an-w an-hero-g"><div class="an-hero-t"><p class="an-k">Rain jackets, decoded</p><h1>Stay dry.<br>Choose the <span>right shell.</span></h1><p class="an-lead">Anoraks is an independent guide to rain jackets. We explain what waterproof and breathability numbers really mean, how to layer for wet weather and how to keep a jacket working for years.</p><p class="an-acts"><a class="btn" href="#tools">Decode my jacket</a><a class="an-ln" href="waterproof-ratings-guide.html">Ratings explained &rarr;</a></p></div><div class="an-hero-i"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1793350660773-3273717a87f0?auto=format&fit=crop&w=640&h=720&q=75" alt="two people in raincoats walking along a waterfront" width="640" height="720" fetchpriority="high"><figure class="an-hero-s"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1650955180071-981236593c0e?auto=format&fit=crop&w=260&h=300&q=75" alt="person in a yellow raincoat holding balloons" width="260" height="300" fetchpriority="high"></figure></div></div></section>

<section class="an-specs"><div class="an-w an-specs-g"><div><b>mm</b><span>Waterproof rating: height of water column the fabric resists</span></div><div><b>g/m&sup2;</b><span>Breathability: moisture let out in 24 hours</span></div><div><b>DWR</b><span>Surface finish that makes rain bead up</span></div><div><b>Seams</b><span>Taped seams stop leaks through stitching</span></div></div></section>

<section class="an-s"><div class="an-w an-spec"><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1708620105068-a0c3c223daec?auto=format&fit=crop&w=540&h=620&q=75" alt="close-up of a person wearing a blue jacket" width="540" height="620" loading="lazy"></figure><div><p class="an-k">Read the tag</p><h2>What makes a jacket waterproof</h2><dl class="an-dl"><div><dt>Face fabric</dt><dd>The outer layer, usually nylon or polyester, treated with DWR.</dd></div><div><dt>Membrane or coating</dt><dd>A thin layer with pores too small for raindrops but large enough for vapour.</dd></div><div><dt>Taped seams</dt><dd>Every stitch hole is a leak unless it is sealed with tape.</dd></div><div><dt>Hood and cuffs</dt><dd>Adjustable openings keep rain out at the edges.</dd></div><div><dt>Vents</dt><dd>Pit zips or mesh pockets let warm, damp air escape.</dd></div></dl></div></div></section>

<section class="an-tl" id="tools"><div class="an-w"><div class="an-th"><p class="an-k">Jacket tools</p><h2>Spec sheet calculators</h2></div><div class="an-tools">
<div class="an-tool"><div class="an-tool-h"><span>T-01</span><h3>Waterproof Rating Decoder</h3></div><label for="wr-m">Rating on the label (mm)<input id="wr-m" type="number" min="0" max="50000" step="500" value="10000"></label><div class="an-out" id="wr-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div>
<div class="an-tool"><div class="an-tool-h"><span>T-02</span><h3>Breathability Decoder</h3></div><label for="br-g">Rating on the label (g/m&sup2;/24h)<input id="br-g" type="number" min="0" max="40000" step="500" value="15000"></label><label for="br-a">Your activity<select id="br-a"><option value="low">Walking around town</option><option value="mid" selected>Hiking</option><option value="high">Running or cycling</option></select></label><div class="an-out" id="br-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div>
<div class="an-tool"><div class="an-tool-h"><span>T-03</span><h3>Rain Layer Picker</h3></div><label for="rl-r">Rain<select id="rl-r"><option value="light">Light showers</option><option value="steady" selected>Steady rain</option><option value="heavy">Heavy, all-day rain</option></select></label><label for="rl-t">Temperature (&deg;C)<input id="rl-t" type="number" min="-20" max="35" value="10"></label><label for="rl-w">Wind<select id="rl-w"><option value="calm">Calm</option><option value="breezy" selected>Breezy</option><option value="strong">Strong</option></select></label><div class="an-out" id="rl-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div>
</div></div></section>

<section class="an-s"><div class="an-w an-lay"><div><p class="an-k">Layering for rain</p><h2>Three layers, one dry day</h2><ol class="an-ol"><li><b>Base</b><span>A wicking top that moves sweat away from your skin. Avoid cotton.</span></li><li><b>Mid</b><span>A light fleece or insulated layer if it is cold; skip it when it is warm.</span></li><li><b>Shell</b><span>Your waterproof jacket, hood up and cuffs closed before the rain gets heavy.</span></li></ol><p>Open the vents before you start sweating, not after. It is easier to stay dry than to dry out.</p></div><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1793296584773-a4327c076136?auto=format&fit=crop&w=540&h=620&q=75" alt="person in a blue hooded jacket hiking with a backpack" width="540" height="620" loading="lazy"></figure></div></section>

<section class="an-s an-pale"><div class="an-w"><div class="an-th"><p class="an-k">Guides</p><h2>Read further</h2></div><div class="an-gds"><a href="waterproof-ratings-guide.html"><div class="an-gi"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1761652003732-80303f51beb5?auto=format&fit=crop&w=640&h=400&q=75" alt="two people in raincoats by a kiosk" width="640" height="400" loading="lazy"></div><div class="an-gt"><span>Guide 01</span><h3>The Waterproof Ratings Guide</h3><p>Hydrostatic head, breathability numbers, membranes and seams, explained without jargon.</p></div></a><a href="rain-jacket-care-guide.html"><div class="an-gi"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1794428101206-7dd46b951659?auto=format&fit=crop&w=640&h=400&q=75" alt="two hikers walking on a grassy mountain slope" width="640" height="400" loading="lazy"></div><div class="an-gt"><span>Guide 02</span><h3>The Rain Jacket Care Guide</h3><p>Washing, reproofing, drying and repairs that keep a shell working for years.</p></div></a></div></div></section>

<section class="an-s"><div class="an-w an-fq"><div class="an-th an-th-l"><p class="an-k">FAQ</p><h2>Wet-weather questions</h2></div><div class="an-faq"><details open><summary>What waterproof rating do I need?</summary><p>For city showers, around 5,000 mm is usually enough. For regular hiking in rain, look for 10,000&ndash;20,000 mm. For long days in heavy rain or mountain weather, 20,000 mm and above.</p></details><details><summary>What is the difference between water-resistant and waterproof?</summary><p>Water-resistant fabrics shed light rain for a while but will eventually soak through. Waterproof jackets use a membrane or coating and taped seams to keep rain out for much longer.</p></details><details><summary>Why do I get wet inside a waterproof jacket?</summary><p>Often it is sweat, not rain. If the jacket cannot let moisture out fast enough, it condenses inside. Opening vents, choosing a more breathable fabric and wearing a wicking layer underneath all help.</p></details><details><summary>What is an anorak?</summary><p>Traditionally, a hooded jacket pulled on over the head, often with a half-length zip and a large front pocket. Today the word is used for many hooded rain and wind jackets.</p></details><details><summary>How often should I wash my rain jacket?</summary><p>Whenever it looks dirty or stops beading water, and at least a few times a season if you use it often. Dirt and body oils reduce both water repellency and breathability.</p></details><details><summary>What is DWR?</summary><p>Durable water repellent: a finish on the outer fabric that makes water bead and roll off. When it wears off, the outer fabric soaks up water and the jacket feels clammy, even though the membrane still keeps rain out.</p></details></div></div></section>

